--- body ---
## Context

Author request, 2026-11-10 ("ticket suggest-attachments"), on a finding of
the documentary-chain survey and of raid f7aac3 (tickets 1502 and 1503).

`zotero.py suggest-attachments --parent K file [--edit]`
(`cmd_suggest_attachments`, ~line 3134 on 2026-11-10; grep again) checks
that item `K` exists and that the file is readable, then, with `--edit`,
stores the file under `K`. Nothing compares the file with the item. The
caller is trusted to have the right pair: `references/backfill.md` tells the
agent to "verify the file belongs to the work before attaching", by hand.

A wrong attachment corrupts the library, and quietly: the item then carries
another work's full text, later lookups by hash point at the wrong item, and
nothing reports it.

The evidence needed is now at hand. Since 1502 and 1503, the helper can read
a file's MD5 against Zotero's own attachment hashes, the identifiers given
in a fetch receipt (`docs/.receipts.jsonl`), the identifiers of a `.bib`
entry, and those printed in the file's pages. `check-staging --apply --edit`
(`attach_present_works`, ~3050) already follows rules about which of these
may license a write (raid f7aac3: a receipt matched only by name gives
hints; a URL match never licenses an attach; a text-scraped identifier never
does). `suggest-attachments` should not be the door that skips them.

## Decision

Before attaching, `suggest-attachments` compares what it can know about the
file with the parent item, and its output row says what it compared:

- **Contradiction refuses.** A given identifier (receipt, `.bib`) that
  differs from the item's own, or the file's MD5 already stored under a
  different item, stops the attach and names both sides.
- **Agreement attaches**, as today, and the row says on which key.
- **Nothing to compare**: the default proposed here is to attach as today
  and mark the row `unverified`, with no new flag, so that the hand-file
  flow keeps working and the report shows where trust was taken. The
  alternative, refusing without an explicit override, is safer and adds a
  step to every hand-supplied file. The author picks; the implementer builds
  the default unless told otherwise.
- The same rules as `attach_present_works` for hints: a text-scraped
  identifier or a name-matched receipt can contradict (and so refuse) but
  cannot, alone, count as agreement.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A file whose given identifier contradicts the parent item's is refused with `--edit`; a test shows the refusal and that the library is unchanged
- [ ] A file whose MD5 is already stored under another item is refused, naming that item; a test shows it
- [ ] A file whose given identifier agrees with the parent attaches, and the row names the key; a test shows it
- [ ] A file with nothing to compare follows the chosen default, and the row says `unverified` (or the refusal says how to override); a test shows it
- [ ] `suggest-attachments` and `check-staging --apply --edit` share one statement of which evidence licenses an attach, in code and in `skills/zotero/references/`
- [ ] The tests that exercise library writes run in the project's gate, or the request says plainly where they are skipped (raid f7aac3 found they skip without a display)
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Plan (raid 13f6d8)

Line numbers are those of 2026-11-10; anchor by text, 1511 shifts them.
The design calls are in the log lines of raid 13f6d8.

### Relevant files

- `scripts/zotero.py`
  - `audit_one`: only the receipt/.bib given-versus-hint block, replaced by a
    call to one extracted helper. The title lines and `filename_only` are
    1511's.
  - A new block just above `TEXT_KEY_ONLY`, the one statement of what licenses
    an attach.
  - `attach_present_works`: its inline `GIVEN_KEYS` test calls that statement;
    behaviour unchanged, no contradiction refusal in the sweep (author ruling).
  - `cmd_suggest_attachments`: comparison on every row with or without
    `--edit`, a refused status, the library lock around index plus attach
    under `--edit`, the exit code.
  - The argparse help of `suggest-attachments` and the module docstring.
- `tests/test_zotero_attach_licence.py` (new): every test of this ticket.
- `tests/test_zotero_import.py`: the test that pins the suggested row, and the
  one that runs the command on a patched `with_zotero` with no client.
- `skills/zotero/references/backfill.md`: a new section on what licenses an
  attach, after "work_present_no_file is repaired by attaching", and the
  "verify the file belongs to the work" sentence. The verdict table and the
  lookup order are 1511's.
- `skills/zotero/SKILL.md`, `skills/zotero/references/helper-script.md`,
  `design/zotero-integration.md` (the `suggest-attachments` and "Upload PDF"
  rows): each points to that section instead of restating it.

### Actions

1. Extract the given-versus-hint block of `audit_one` into a helper both
   commands call (.bib identifiers are given; a receipt's count as given only
   when it was found by md5 and the .bib gives none; otherwise hints). Pure
   refactor: the existing receipt, staging-identity and check-staging tests
   pass unchanged. One commit.
2. The licensing test on a row's `why` labels becomes one function beside
   `GIVEN_KEYS`; `attach_present_works` calls it.
3. What the parent holds, per identifier kind, read as the `carries` lambdas
   of `IDENTIFIER_KEYS` read it (DOI field and Extra `DOI:` lines; ISBN field
   and Extra, never for a parent type in `ISBN_OF_A_VOLUME`; arXiv; handle).
   A kind the parent does not hold is nothing to compare. Do not change the
   shape of `IDENTIFIER_KEYS`.
4. A pure decision function: another holder of the file's md5 refuses and
   names that item; a given identifier whose kind the parent holds with a
   different value refuses, names both values and says how to proceed
   (record the second identifier on the item, rerun); a hint or a
   text-scraped identifier may refuse the same way, its message saying it may
   be a cited work's, and never counts as agreement; a given identifier that
   agrees attaches and the row names the key; anything else is `unverified`.
5. The .bib entry of a file: the project root is the nearest ancestor of the
   file holding `.git` or `.zotero-reconcile.json`, never cwd; then the
   discovery `check-staging` uses. No root or a discovery error goes into the
   row's `skipped` and blocks nothing.
6. The evidence of one file against one parent: the parent's `work_record`,
   the claims of step 1 (text identifiers only when no given one exists), the
   parent's own copy through `file_evidence` (no index: `already stored`,
   nothing written), other holders through `find_work(pdf_path=,
   md5_index=)` on an index of the parent's own library, built once per run.
   An index that cannot be built: the row says the md5 comparison was skipped
   and why, the attach proceeds, the exit code is 1 (74 and 75 mean "nothing
   was saved" and would lie).
7. Reference text and help.
8. The request states which tests are live (`integration`), that they skip
   with "no display" under `make check`, that the gate is not widened here,
   and quotes one run with a display.

### Test

- First, red before any code, in `tests/test_zotero_attach_licence.py`
  (`integration`, the live fixture as `tests/test_zotero_receipt.py` uses it):
  a parent with one DOI, a PDF whose md5-matched receipt gives another DOI,
  `suggest-attachments --edit`. Asserts: exit 1, row refused, both DOIs in the
  error, the parent has no child, every item version unchanged. Today: exit 0
  and the file attached, an AssertionError on behaviour.
- Each case with a control differing by one fact:
  - Criterion 1: the same file attaches once the parent's Extra carries the
    second DOI.
  - Criterion 2: the file stored under item X and aimed at another parent is
    refused naming X, the parent's children unchanged; different bytes attach.
    Also: already stored under the parent itself writes nothing; a report-only
    run shows the refusal and writes nothing.
  - Criterion 3: an agreeing receipt DOI attaches, the row names `doi`; a
    receipt matched by name only with the same DOI is `unverified`; with a
    different DOI it refuses; a `.bib` entry found from the file with cwd
    elsewhere refuses on a differing DOI.
  - Criterion 4: no receipt, no .bib, a parent with no identifier attaches
    `unverified`; a skipped index attaches and the row says so.
- "Library unchanged" is read back from the client (item versions, children),
  never from the exit code or the row.
- Fast tier, same file: a truth table of the decision function on literal
  identifiers (second DOI in Extra, volume ISBN on a chapter, hint only,
  scraped mismatch), and the licensing labels.
- Existing tests that change: the pinned suggested row gains the comparison;
  the no-client test patches the one evidence seam and keeps its
  batch-stops-on-refused-key assertions.
- By hand, since live tests skip with no display:
  `DISPLAY=:0 uv run --frozen python3 -m pytest
  tests/test_zotero_attach_licence.py -m integration -v -rs` (the client draws
  on its own private Xvfb); a SKIPPED line means the run proved nothing.

### Collisions with 1511

1511 edits the title lines and `filename_only` in `audit_one`,
`backfill.md`'s verdict table and lookup order, and tests in
`test_zotero_check_staging_bib.py` and `test_zotero_find_work.py`. No shared
hunk; whoever lands second rebases.
